The '''Disgrace of Gijón''' ({{Langx|es|Desgracia de Gijón}}) is one of many names given in hindsight to the [[1982 FIFA World Cup]] football match played at the [[El Molinón|El Molinón stadium]] in [[Gijón]], Spain, on 25 June 1982, in which [[West Germany national football team|West Germany]] beat [[Austria national football team|Austria]] 1–0. Both teams advanced to the next round while [[Algeria national football team|Algeria]] was eliminated by having the lowest goal difference. Algeria had played its last game a day earlier, unable to achieve the four-goal victory margin that would have seen them through regardless of what happened in this match. In [[German language|German]], the match is known as {{lang|de|Nichtangriffspakt von Gijón}} ({{literal translation}} "[[Non-aggression pact]] of Gijón") or {{lang|de|Schande von Gijón}} ({{literal translation}} "Disgrace of Gijón"),<ref name="GuardianWorldCup">{{cite news |last=Smyth |first=Rob |date=25 February 2014 |title=No3: West Germany 1–0 Austria in 1982 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2014/feb/25/world-cup-25-stunning-moments-no3-germany-austria-1982-rob-smyth |access-date=23 June 2014 |newspaper=The Guardian |archive-date=27 February 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190227072056/https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2014/feb/25/world-cup-25-stunning-moments-no3-germany-austria-1982-rob-smyth |url-status=live }}</ref> while in [[Algeria]] it is called {{lang|ar|فضيحة خيخون}} ({{transliteration|ar|faḍīḥat Khīkhūn}}, "Scandal of Gijón"), and in [[French language|French]] {{lang|fr|le Match de la honte}} (lit. "The Shameful Match"); it has also been satirically referred to as the {{lang|de|[[Anschluss]]}} (a reference to the [[Nazi German]] annexation of Austria in 1938).<ref>{{cite book |last=Spurling |first=Jon |title=Death or Glory The Dark History of the World Cup |publisher=Vision Sports |year=2010 |isbn=978-1905326-80-8 |page=67}}</ref>

The Algerian team had beaten two-time World Champions and reigning European Champions West Germany 2–1 in the first round, then had lost to Austria 2–0, before beating Chile 3–2. As the last game of the first-round [[1982 FIFA World Cup Group 2|Group 2]] was scheduled a day later, the two German-speaking teams had an advantage in knowing that a West German win by fewer than three goals would qualify both, while any other result would see either Austria or West Germany eliminated. After a German goal early in the game, the match remained competitive before the teams started to "defend" the 1–0 score in the second half, as there was little benefit for either side to try harder. Many spectators, mostly Algerians, were unhappy with the perceived lack of effort, and accused the West German and Austrian teams of manipulation.<ref name="GuardianWorldCup"/>

Despite widespread condemnation and a formal complaint lodged by Algeria – who were consequently eliminated – [[FIFA]] ruled that neither team had broken any rules. Following the tournament – with similar controversy regarding [[Argentina v Peru (1978 FIFA World Cup)|Argentina vs Peru]] in the [[1978 FIFA World Cup|previous World Cup]] – FIFA revised the group system for future competitions so that the final two games in each group would be played simultaneously.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Booth|first1=Lawrence|last2=Smyth|first2=Rob|title=What's the dodgiest game in football history?|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2004/aug/11/theknowledge.sport|newspaper=[[The Guardian]]|location=London|date=11 August 2004|access-date=16 June 2018|archive-date=14 December 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131214113736/http://www.theguardian.com/football/2004/aug/11/theknowledge.sport|url-status=live}}</ref> This change made it highly impractical for teams to engage in any form of [[Match fixing in association football|match-fixing]], since they would not know what result was required ahead of time.

