==Aftermath==
{{:1982 FIFA World Cup Group 2|transcludesection=standings}}
With West Germany's 1–0 victory, they joined Austria and Algeria with four points in three matches. The teams were separated by goal difference, with West Germany and Austria progressing to the next round of the tournament at the expense of Algeria. The [[match-fixing]] saw Austria give up their opportunity to be first in the group (by winning or drawing the match) in exchange for a sure opportunity to advance. The bargaining positions of the two teams were affected by West Germany being in danger of elimination if they failed to win, but also being the better team.<ref>{{citation|url=http://mpra.ub.uni-muenchen.de/3085/1/MPRA_paper_3085.pdf|title=The Economics of Match-Fixing|first=R|last=Caruso|date=2007|access-date=23 June 2010|archive-date=11 April 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180411114019/https://mpra.ub.uni-muenchen.de/3085/1/MPRA_paper_3085.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref> By coming second in the group, Austria's second-stage group was France and Northern Ireland. West Germany's opponents were hosts Spain and England who had previously beaten France. Also, for three of the starting players ([[Horst Hrubesch]] for West Germany, [[Josef Degeorgi]] and [[Roland Hattenberger]] for Austria), an additional incentive to avoid aggressive play was that they had been previously booked in their respective teams' first two games. Under the rules then in force, an additional yellow card for any of them in the final group match would have resulted in them serving an automatic one-match ban to start the second round.

After the match, the West German team went back to their hotel where they were met by furious spectators who threw eggs and other projectiles at them; the West Germans returned fire with water bombs.<ref>{{cite news|title=The Disgrace of Gijon: West Germany and Austria's match of shame at 1982 World Cup|url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/3643998/2022/11/15/west-germany-austria-disgrace-of-gijon-world-cup/|publisher=[[The Athletic]]|date=15 November 2022|access-date=26 April 2024|archive-date=20 June 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240620234024/https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/3643998/2022/11/15/west-germany-austria-disgrace-of-gijon-world-cup/|url-status=live}}</ref> German and Austrian television commentators were so appalled at the match that they urged viewers on live television to stop watching the match and watch something else. The Algerian football officials lodged an official protest. In addition, the president of the [[Algerian Football Federation]] opined that referee [[Bob Valentine (referee)|Bob Valentine]] should have intervened and his failure to do so was worthy of complaint;<ref>{{cite news|title=Cup game labeled as 'fix'|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=jvVVAAAAIBAJ&sjid=W-IDAAAAIBAJ&pg=6088%2C6157584|newspaper=[[The Register-Guard]]|location=Eugene|date=26 June 1982|access-date=24 July 2014|archive-date=24 September 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240924032119/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=jvVVAAAAIBAJ&sjid=W-IDAAAAIBAJ&pg=6088,6157584|url-status=live}}</ref> however, FIFA considered that no rules were broken as a result of the match, and declined to take any action. Both teams denied any collusion during the match.<ref name="CBCWorldCup">{{cite news|last=Molinaro|first=John|title=No agreement between Germany and Austria this time around|url=https://www.cbc.ca/sports/soccer/no-agreement-between-germany-and-austria-this-time-around-1.766773|publisher=[[CBC Sports]]|date=16 June 2008|access-date=15 September 2009}}</ref> West Germany manager [[Jupp Derwall]] defended his team from the criticism, pointing out that Uli Stielike and Karl-Heinz Rummenigge were both unfit.<ref>{{cite news|title=German victory in World Cup stirs controversy|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=4h8qAAAAIBAJ&sjid=4CkEAAAAIBAJ&pg=4793%2C2674558|archive-url=https://archive.today/20180711112248/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=4h8qAAAAIBAJ&sjid=4CkEAAAAIBAJ&pg=4793,2674558|url-status=dead|archive-date=11 July 2018|agency=Associated Press; United Press International|newspaper=[[Milwaukee Journal]]|date=26 June 1982|access-date=24 July 2014|page=10}}</ref> The West Germans made it to [[1982 FIFA World Cup Final|the final]], where they lost to [[Italy national football team|Italy]] 3–1. Austria fell at the next group stage, to the benefit of eventual fourth-place finishers [[France national football team|France]]. As a result of the game, from the [[1986 FIFA World Cup|World Cup 1986]] onward, the final pair of group matches in World Cups always start simultaneously.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.algeria.com/blog/the-game-that-changed-the-world-cup-algeria|title=The Game that Changed the World Cup&nbsp;— Algeria|work=algeria.com|access-date=15 September 2009|archive-date=10 September 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150910220511/http://www.algeria.com/blog/the-game-that-changed-the-world-cup-algeria|url-status=live}}</ref>

Later journalists took another look at the match, wondering if claims of "non-aggression" are exaggerated. In ''[[The Irish Times]]'', Rob Smyth wrote: "The 10 minutes after Hrubesch's goal would even be described as exhilarating in some cultures, with [[Wolfgang Dremmler]] forcing a fine save from [[Friedrich Koncilia]] (the second and final shot on target in the match) and [[Paul Breitner]] missing two good chances. The game slows down towards half-time, principally because the hitherto dominant Germany start to play on the [[counterattack]]. Hrubesch would have had a clear shooting chance in the 57th minute had he not hopelessly miscontrolled [[Felix Magath]]’s expert chip. As late as the 77th minute, when the game was losing what edge it had, [[Bernd Krauss]] broke into the box and forced a desperate clearance from [[Hans-Peter Briegel]]. A goal then would have put West Germany out."<ref name="smyth">{{Cite news|url=https://www.irishtimes.com/sport/soccer/international/world-cup-moments-1982-s-disgrace-of-gij%C3%B3n-1.3479123|title=World Cup moments: 1982's 'Disgrace of Gijón'|newspaper=[[The Irish Times]]|archive-date=24 September 2024|access-date=6 January 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240924032119/https://www.irishtimes.com/sport/soccer/international/world-cup-moments-1982-s-disgrace-of-gij%C3%B3n-1.3479123|url-status=live}}</ref> Almost all participants denied an outright agreement to fix the match. Austrian player Reinhold Hintermaier described the situation on the pitch as follows: "It somehow took on a life of its own – everyone just thought that they wanted to get ahead."<ref name="Hintermaier">{{Cite web |date=2022-06-05 |title="Schande von Gijon": Lasst uns das nach Hause schaukeln |trans-title="Disgrace of Gijón": Let's Drive This Home |last=Münchrath |first=Manfred |url=https://www.kicker.de/schande-von-gijon_lasst-uns-das-nach-hause-schaukeln-700331/artikel |access-date=2023-07-01 |website=kicker |language=de |archive-date=19 March 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210319233108/https://www.kicker.de/schande-von-gijon_lasst-uns-das-nach-hause-schaukeln-700331/artikel |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Similar cases in football==
* [[Coventry City 2–2 Bristol City (1977)|Coventry City 2–2 Bristol City]], the final match for both sides in the [[1976–77 Football League|1976–77]] season of [[The Football League|English First Division]]. With five minutes remaining, players received news of a [[Sunderland A.F.C.|Sunderland]] defeat which meant a draw would save both Coventry and Bristol City from relegation. Both sides stopped attempting to score for those final five minutes.<ref>[https://www.bristolpost.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/day-bristol-city-game-led-66455 The day a Bristol City game led to claims of match fixing, a bitter 40-year feud and a reprimand for a famous football personality] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210507204506/https://www.bristolpost.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/day-bristol-city-game-led-66455 |date=7 May 2021 }} by Philippa Jenkins
05:00, 19 May 2017 at bristolpost.co.uk. 19 May 2017</ref>

* [[2002 FIFA World Cup qualification (CONMEBOL)#Matchday 18|Uruguay 1–1 Argentina]], played during the final matchday of CONMEBOL's qualification for the [[2002 FIFA World Cup]]. Argentina had already secured qualification well in advance. On November 14, 2001, the national team visited the [[Estadio Centenario]] to play against [[Uruguay national football team|Uruguay]], who needed to earn a point and hope that [[Colombia national football team|Colombia]] did not beat [[Paraguay national football team|Paraguay]] by a margin of five goals or more in order to be in the playoff that would allow them to access the World Cup. The match ended 1–1 and Uruguay therefore secured their place in the next round against [[Australia men's national soccer team|Australia]], despite Colombia's 4–0 victory over Paraguay.<ref name=tyc1/> The match was repeatedly accused by some of being fixed, as there were few attacks from either side in the final fifteen minutes.<ref name=tyc1>[https://www.tycsports.com/eliminatorias/el-argentina-uruguay-sospechado-de-arregla-en-las-eliminatorias-que-enfurecio-a-bielsa-id512079.html El Argentina - Uruguay sospechado de arreglo en las Eliminatorias que enfureció a Bielsa] at tycsports.com.ar</ref> In 2004, Uruguayan [[Juan Ramón Carrasco]] confirmed a pact between both sides to allow Uruguay qualify to the World Cup, saying "it's common knowledge. Everyone here knows that they fixed the match to draw because Argentina had already qualified".<ref name=infob1>[https://www.infobae.com/2004/10/06/144250-confirman-partido-arreglado-argentina-y-uruguay/ Confirman partido arreglado entre Argentina y Uruguay] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260605211540/https://www.infobae.com/2004/10/06/144250-confirman-partido-arreglado-argentina-y-uruguay/ |date=5 June 2026 }} at infobae.com. 6 Oct 2004</ref>

* [[2018 FIFA World Cup qualification (CONMEBOL)#Matchday 18|Peru 1–1 Colombia]], also known as the ''Pact of Lima'', played during the final matchday of CONMEBOL's qualification for the [[2018 FIFA World Cup]]. After reaching the 1–1 scoreline and learning that Chile (a rival for both in qualifying hopes) was losing 2–0 in Brazil, Peruvians and Colombians stopped attempting to score for the last fifteen minutes of the match.<ref>{{cite news |title=El recuerdo del 'Pacto de Lima' en Perú vs Colombia por las Eliminatorias al Mundial 2018 |url=https://www.espn.co.cr/futbol/mundial/nota/_/id/14128835/el-recuerdo-del-pacto-de-lima-en-peru-vs-colombia-por-las-eliminatorias-al-mundial-2018 |access-date=7 December 2025 |work=ESPN Costa Rica |date=4 September 2024 |language=es}}</ref> Colombia qualified directly, and Peru advanced to the [[2018 FIFA World Cup qualification (OFC–CONMEBOL play-off)|inter-confederation play-offs]], where they defeated New Zealand to qualify. Chile, who conceded a third goal by the Brazilians, were eliminated.
* [[2018 FIFA World Cup Group H#Japan vs Poland|Japan 0–1 Poland]] — In the third group stage match of the [[2018 FIFA World Cup]], [[Japan national football team|Japan]] was losing 0–1 with 10 minutes to go. After seeing the situation in the other group stage match being held simultaneously, Senegal vs. Colombia, they realized that they could advance to the next round if they lost by the same score. At that point, Japan abandoned any attempt to equalize or win, and simply passed the ball around at the back. Poland also stopped trying to win the ball back and played listless football. They were booed in anger by the crowd.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2018-06-28 |title=World Cup 2018: Japan go through but final group game ends in 'mind-boggling farce' |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/44649668 |access-date=2026-06-12 |work=BBC Sport |language=en-GB |archive-date=5 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180705152823/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/44649668 |url-status=live }}</ref>

* The [[2024 AFC Women's Olympic Qualifying Tournament]] match between [[Uzbekistan women's national football team|Uzbekistan]] and [[Japan women's national football team|Japan]], which ended in a 0–2 result, drew controversy due to allegations of match manipulation. Both teams, led by Japanese coaches, appeared to benefit from the outcome: Japan avoided a potential playoff against [[Australia women's national soccer team|Australia]], a formidable opponent, while Uzbekistan maintained a chance for a playoff berth due to the low scoreline. Japan scored two goals within the first 15 minutes, after which both teams exhibited notably passive play for the remaining 75 minutes, with no shots attempted by either side. This result effectively eliminated [[China women's national football team|China]] from contention. Japan subsequently qualified for the [[2024 Summer Olympics]] by defeating [[North Korea women's national football team|North Korea]] in a playoff match.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://news.jsports.co.jp/football/article/20190310226081/ |title=ウズベキスタン相手に75分間の無気力サッカー なでしこジャパンの将来に、どんな意味があったのか |website=J SPORTS |language=ja |access-date=2024-08-31 |archive-date=31 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240831171042/https://news.jsports.co.jp/football/article/20190310226081/ |url-status=live }}</ref> {{cns|Japan set a new world record for passes in this match: 1,466.|date=July 2026}} This significantly surpasses the previous record of 1,033 passes, set by [[Bayern Munich]] in a [[Bundesliga]] match in 2014.<ref name=guard_pass>{{Cite news |last=sport |first=Guardian |date=2019-09-04 |title=Which football team have completed the most passes in a match? |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/sep/04/which-football-team-have-completed-the-most-passes-in-a-match |access-date=2026-05-20 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> The crowd demanded that both teams stop the match-fixing. However, both teams ignored them and continued playing listless football. When the second half started, both teams continued playing listless football without any change. As a result, the crowd became angry and began to leave in droves within the first few minutes of the second half, with 1500 out of 2000 leaving.<ref>{{Cite web |title=サッカー日本代表 - 女子五輪2次予選 第2節 ウズベキスタン vs. 日本 - 試合テキスト速報 |url=https://soccer.yahoo.co.jp/japan/category/women/game/2023102917/text?gk=32 |access-date=2026-05-20 |website=スポーツナビ |language=ja}}</ref>
* [[2026 FIFA World Cup Group J#Algeria vs Austria|Algeria 3–3 Austria]]. Two of the teams involved in the original Disgrace of Gijón next met in the final set of games of the group stage of [[2026 FIFA World Cup|2026 World Cup]], 44 years later. Due to the results of the other groups, both teams entered the game knowing that a draw would see them both qualify, with Austria in second place and Algeria as one of the eight best third-placed teams, at the expense of [[Iran national football team]]; had either team lost, Iran would have qualified in the place of the loser.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Mezahi |first=Maher |date=2026-06-26 |title=Algeria eye revenge in first match against Austria since ‘shame of Gijón’ |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/jun/26/algeria-austria-1982-world-cup-shame-of-gijon-west-germany |access-date=2026-06-28 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> Despite the spectre of Gijón — the media had been concerned about a potential "Disgrace of Kansas City" before the match<ref>{{Cite web |title= 'Disgrace of Kansas City' loading? Austria-Algeria clash set to expose World Cup's unfair format - and that could play straight into Gianni Infantino's hands |url= https://www.goal.com/en-gb/lists/disgrace-kansas-city-austria-algeria-expose-unfair-world-cup-format-fifa-gianni-infantino/blt6eca28fc7d7c2381 |access-date= 2026-06-28 |website= Goal.com |language= en |archive-date= 30 June 2026 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260630140225/https://www.goal.com/en-gb/lists/disgrace-kansas-city-austria-algeria-expose-unfair-world-cup-format-fifa-gianni-infantino/blt6eca28fc7d7c2381 |url-status= live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Is the World Cup About to Witness the ‘Disgrace of Kansas City’?|URL=https://www.wsj.com/sports/soccer/is-the-world-cup-about-to-witness-the-disgrace-of-kansas-city-981dd863|website=[[WSJ]]|access-date=2026-06-28|archive-date=28 June 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260628134210/https://www.wsj.com/sports/soccer/is-the-world-cup-about-to-witness-the-disgrace-of-kansas-city-981dd863|url-status=live}}</ref> — Austria and Algeria played to a spirited 3–3 draw. The score was tied at 2–2 in the second half, with the teams starting to play more cautiously, before a goal for each team in [[injury time]] proving decisive for both countries' further qualification.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Tansley |first=Eduardo |date=2026-06-28 |title=The goal that sparked chaos: 110 passes, Mahrez’s finish, Austria panic, Iran woe |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7403358/2026/06/28/world-cup-algeria-mahrez-austria-iran/ |access-date=2026-06-30 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=16 July 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260716052619/https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7403358/2026/06/28/world-cup-algeria-mahrez-austria-iran/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Abnos |first=Alexander |date=2026-06-28 |title=Late drama sends Austria and Algeria into World Cup knockouts to break Iran hearts |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/jun/28/algeria-austria-world-cup-group-j-match-report |access-date=2026-06-28 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> In post-match interviews, both coaches denied there had been any collusion.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-06-28 |title=World Cup 2026: Algeria & Austria defend 'unexpected' draw amid conspiracy theories |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/ckg4v1wvwxxo |access-date=2026-06-30 |website=BBC Sport |language=en-GB |archive-date=30 June 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260630144450/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/ckg4v1wvwxxo |url-status=live }}</ref>

